 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 52,
   "plaque": "belfast_chaim_herzog",
   "title": "Chaim Herzog",
   "text": "Chaim Herzog was born in Belfast in 1918, where his father Yitzhak Herzog was the city's rabbi before becoming Chief Rabbi of Ireland. Chaim grew up in Dublin, fought in the British Army in the Second World War and became the sixth President of Israel in 1983.",
   "people": "jewish"
  },
  {
   "text": "THE LAGAN. Belfast sits where this river meets the sea, and the name 'Béal Feirste' means 'mouth of the sandbank ford'. There used to be a ford here. Now there are bridges, a weir, and a very large metal fish.",
   "x": 39.0,
   "y": 32.0
  },
  {
   "text": "BELFAST CITY HALL. Opened in 1906 in Portland stone, with a copper dome that went green long ago. The grounds are open to everyone and have been used for fairs, concerts, and the city's grandest sulks.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 17.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE PALM HOUSE. A curved glass and iron greenhouse from the 1830s, one of the oldest in the world. It was built to keep tropical plants alive in a climate that disagrees. The plants appear to have won.",
   "x": 16.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE LINEN HALL LIBRARY. Founded in 1788, the oldest library in Belfast, with a very patient librarian. Belfast once made so much linen it was nicknamed Linenopolis.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 21.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ALBERT CLOCK. A tower from the 1860s built on wooden piles in soft ground. It leans a little, like a clock that has been up too late. It still tells the time, usually correctly.",
   "x": 38.0,
   "y": 27.0
  },
  {
   "text": "TITANIC BELFAST. A museum opened in 2012, its front like the prows of four ships, as tall as the Titanic's hull. The ship was built on the slipway here at Harland and Wolff. This is where the story starts.",
   "x": 53.0,
   "y": 28.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE SLIPWAYS. Two long slopes, each as big as a football pitch, where the biggest ships of their day slid into the water. The hulls took years to build and seconds to launch, and they were greased with tallow. Lots of tallow.",
   "x": 47.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "SAMSON AND GOLIATH. The shipyard's two giant yellow cranes: Goliath, 96 metres, built in 1969, and Samson, 106 metres, in 1974. You can see them from almost anywhere in Belfast.",
   "x": 51.0,
   "y": 41.0
  },
  {
   "text": "BELFAST PORT. The ferry to Cairnryan in Scotland leaves from here. The crossing takes a couple of hours, the sea is as grey as the sky, and the sandwiches are optimistic.",
   "x": 60.0,
   "y": 36.0
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: C. S. Lewis, author of The Chronicles of Narnia, was born in Belfast on 29 November 1898.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "belfast_cs_lewis",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "C. S. Lewis"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 51,
   "plaque": "belfast_harland_wolff",
   "title": "Harland and Wolff",
   "text": "In 1858 Edward Harland bought a small shipyard on Queen's Island, and in 1861 he took on Gustav Wolff, a young engineer from Hamburg, as his partner. Their yard grew into one of the biggest in the world. The name is still painted on Samson and Goliath."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "belfast_lord_kelvin",
   "title": "Lord Kelvin",
   "text": "William Thomson was born in Belfast in 1824 and became Lord Kelvin, one of the great scientists of his age. He helped lay the first working telegraph cable under the Atlantic, and the kelvin, the scientific unit of temperature, is named after him. Absolute zero is very cold. He worked it out anyway."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "belfast_dunlop_tyre",
   "title": "Dunlop's Tyre",
   "text": "In 1887 John Boyd Dunlop, a Scottish vet working in Belfast, wrapped an air-filled rubber tube round the wheels of his son's tricycle to smooth out the bumpy streets. He patented the pneumatic tyre in 1888. Every bicycle that has ever had a puncture owes him a grumble."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 4,
   "plaque": "belfast_cave_hill",
   "title": "Cave Hill",
   "text": "The hill above the city has a craggy profile locals call Napoleon's Nose, and is said to have helped inspire the sleeping giant in Gulliver's Travels. In 1795 Wolfe Tone and fellow United Irishmen climbed to McArt's Fort on the summit and swore an oath to work for Irish independence."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "WEST: Lough Neagh, the biggest lake in these islands, and on to Enniskillen and the Fermanagh lakes. Fermanagh folk will tell you it's the best county. Everyone else will tell you it's the wettest."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "arts_harp_festival_1792",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Belfast Harp Festival",
   "text": "In July 1792 the harpers of Ireland, ten of them, many old and several blind, were brought together at the Assembly Rooms in Belfast to play before the old music was lost. A nineteen-year-old organist, Edward Bunting, sat with pen and paper and wrote the tunes down as they played; Denis Hempson of Magilligan, nearly a hundred, still plucked with long fingernails in the ancient style. Bunting's General Collection of the Ancient Irish Music came out in 1796, and most of what we know of the old harp music comes from that week. The harpers were paid. Not enough."
  }
 ],
